.TH "MYD" "1" "July 2026" "myd 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
myd \- vi-like terminal file browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmyd\fR [\fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmyd\fR is a terminal user interface (TUI) for browsing and managing the
filesystem. It provides a directory tree with \fBvi\fR-style key bindings,
a live info panel showing file metadata, and proportional size bars that
visualize disk usage at a glance.
.PP
Built with \fBratatui\fR and \fBcrossterm\fR.
.PP
If \fIPATH\fR is provided, the browser starts at that directory; otherwise it
starts in the current directory. The directory picker is reached with \fBg d\fR.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.SS Navigation
.TP
.B j
Move cursor down one line.
.TP
.B k
Move cursor up one line.
.TP
.B h
In the tree view, collapse the current directory or move the cursor to the
parent entry. In the treemap view, move the cursor one tile to the left; when
already on a left-edge tile, step up to the parent directory.
.TP
.B l
In the tree view, expand the current directory. In the treemap view, move the
cursor one tile to the right.
.TP
.B gg
Jump to the first line of the tree.
.TP
.B G
Jump to the last line of the tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl+f
Move down a full screen. The PageDown key does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+b
Move up a full screen. The PageUp key does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+d
Move down half a screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl+u
Move up half a screen.
.TP
.B g u
Move cursor to the parent directory.
.TP
.B g d
Prompt for a new root directory and navigate to it. The picker opens over the
current view; \fBEsc\fR dismisses it and returns to where you were.
.IP
The picker lists local directories and saved remote hosts together, in two
labelled sections \(em both answer "where do you want to go", and keeping two
near-identical screens meant two places to fix every bug. \fBEnter\fR opens a
directory or connects to a host, whichever the highlighted row is. \fBg s\fR
opens the same list narrowed to hosts.
.IP
It has two halves: a path field and the list.
\fBTab\fR moves the keyboard between them, and the focused one is outlined in
cyan. With the field focused, type a path (\fB~\fR is expanded) and press
\fBEnter\fR. With the list focused, \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR move through the
entries. The arrow keys reach the list from the field too: the first press
selects the entry already highlighted rather than stepping past it, and further
presses move. \fBHome\fR, \fBEnd\fR and the left/right arrows hand the
keyboard back to the field.
.IP
Selecting an entry offers its path in the field, and typing then replaces that
offer \(em press \fBEnd\fR first to extend it instead, which is how you append
a subdirectory to a listed path.
.IP
Every directory opened through this dialog is remembered, whether typed or
chosen from the list, so a path typed once is a keystroke away afterwards.
Directories reached by browsing the tree are not: the list is for destinations
you chose, and recording every directory drilled into would bury them.
.IP
With the list focused, \fBa\fR prompts for a
destination to keep \(em a path, or a \fBlabel = sftp://…\fR line to save a
host \(em and \fBd\fR forgets the highlighted one. \fBe\fR edits the highlighted
entry \(em a directory's path, or a host's details \(em in a popup, keeping its
visit history and its place in the pinned block rather than making a correction
mean deleting and re-adding. \fBS\fR turns measuring on or off for the
highlighted directory without opening it \(em marking somewhere known to be slow
should not require paying for the walk first \(em and the row shows
\fB(shallow)\fR when it is off. \fB/\fR searches the whole list (\fBEsc\fR
clears it).
\fB/\fR only starts a search when the path field is empty, since that is also
how most absolute paths begin.
.IP
Remembered directories are stored in the \fB[[favorite]]\fR section of
\fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR beside the saved hosts, and shown in three
groups: pinned entries (\fB\(ua\fR) at the top in an order you arrange, saved
entries (\fB\(*\fR) next, then automatically remembered ones.
.IP
A catalog with no directories in it \(em a first run, or a file that only ever
held hosts \(em is seeded with the usual locations (home, Desktop, Documents,
Downloads, Pictures, Music, Videos, / and /tmp) and written out. They are
ordinary entries from then on: pin, reorder and delete them like any other. A
file that already lists directories is left alone, and a file that does not
parse is never written over. Within the last two groups the order is most-recently-visited first,
so the places you actually use rise to the top.
.IP
\fBp\fR pins the highlighted entry to the \fIbottom\fR of the pinned block \(em
the block is an order you arranged, so a new pin does not barge into the middle
of it \(em and \fBu\fR takes it out again, keeping the entry. \fBm\fR starts a
reorder: \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR (or the arrows) reposition the entry, \fBEnter\fR
confirms and \fBEsc\fR puts it back where it started. \fBm\fR on an entry that
is not yet pinned pins it first, so positioning something never needs two keys.
.IP
Sliding an entry one step past the bottom of the block takes it out: the row's
marker changes as it leaves, so what \fBEnter\fR will do is visible before it
is committed, and \fBk\fR brings it back in.
.IP
Automatically remembered directories are trimmed to the most recent twenty.
Anything saved with \fBa\fR or pinned with \fBp\fR is never trimmed.
.TP
.B 0
Collapse all expanded directories.
.TP
.B *
Expand all directories.
.SS File Operations
.TP
.B D
Delete the tagged files, or the selected one when nothing is tagged (with a
confirmation dialog). Directories are removed recursively.
.TP
.B R
Rename the currently selected file or directory. An existing name is never
overwritten.
.TP
.B N
Create a new directory in the current location.
.TP
.B c
Copy the tagged files, or the selected one, into the other panel's directory.
.TP
.B m
Move the tagged files, or the selected one, into the other panel's directory.
Within a single filesystem this is a rename, so it completes instantly whatever
the size; between two different hosts the bytes are copied and the source is
removed only after that file's copy succeeds. A cross-host move is queued and
reported in the transfer panel exactly like a copy.
.IP
When a name is already taken at the destination, myd asks what to do: overwrite
it, skip that file and continue, or cancel the whole move. Cancelling abandons
every remaining file, including ones that would not have collided.
.TP
.B r
Rescan: clear the size cache and re-read sizes from disk. Sizes are otherwise
cached and reused as you navigate, so this is how to pick up on-disk changes.
.SS View
.TP
.B v
Toggle between the tree view and the treemap view, carrying the selection over
so the entry you were looking at in one is the one highlighted in the other. The
chosen view persists as you navigate between directories.
.IP
\fBEnter\fR opens the selected directory in either view. Treemap tiles for
directories end in a slash, as in
.BR ls (1),
so a tile says whether there is anything to open.
.TP
.B Ctrl+p
Toggle the info panel sidebar. Hidden by default.
.TP
.B s
Cycle through sort modes: largest, smallest, dirs-first, files-first, newest
(modification time), oldest (modification time), and recently accessed (access
time).
.TP
.B H
Toggle visibility of hidden (dot) files and directories.
.TP
.B b
Toggle display of proportional size bars in the tree.
.TP
.B P
Toggle an
.BR ls (1)-style
permissions column, shown to the left of each entry's name. Entries whose
backend does not report permissions show
.BR ?--------- .
.TP
.B T
Toggle a modification-time column, shown to the left of each entry's name.
Entries with no reported time show a dash.
.TP
.B /
Search for a file or directory by name. The pattern is a case-insensitive
regular expression; \fBn\fR and \fBp\fR step to the next and previous match,
wrapping at the ends.
.TP
.B f
Filter the tree by a case-insensitive regular expression: entries whose names
do not match are hidden, at every level. A directory is kept when something
beneath it matches, so matching files stay reachable. The filter is a view mask
\(em the tree itself is untouched \(em and an empty pattern clears it.
.IP
While a filter is active the title bar reads \fBFILTERED\fR and reports how
many entries are \fIshown\fR rather than how many exist, and the footer carries
the pattern \(em a masked view is never mistaken for the directory's real
contents. On a narrow terminal the footer badge drops its hint, then the
pattern, but never the fact that filtering is on.
.IP
A pattern that is not valid regular expression syntax is reported. Note that
\fB*p$\fR is not valid (there is nothing for \fB*\fR to repeat); the pattern
for "names ending in p" is \fBp$\fR, or \fB.*p$\fR.
.TP
.B ? / F1
Show or hide the key reference. It is taller than a typical terminal, so
\fBj\fR / \fBk\fR, the arrow keys, PageUp/PageDown, \fBg\fR / \fBG\fR and the
mouse wheel scroll it; the title bar shows your position in the list.
.SS Screen Level
.TP
.B q
Quit. With transfers still queued or running this asks for confirmation first,
since abandoning one discards a partial copy. \fBCtrl+C\fR force-quits without
asking.
.TP
.B Esc
Quit, with the same confirmation as \fBq\fR.
.TP
.B Ctrl+o
Go back to the parent directory.
.TP
.B o
Open the selected file or directory with the system's default application \(em
.BR open (1)
on macOS,
.BR xdg\-open (1)
elsewhere. The launcher is started and myd carries on; it is not waited for, and
its output is discarded so it cannot corrupt the display.
.IP
Remote entries are refused: those launchers only understand local paths, and a
remote path either fails or opens an unrelated local file that happens to share
it. Copy the file across first with \fBc\fR.
.TP
.B S
Browse without measuring directory sizes, and back again.
.IP
Measuring means a recursive walk, which is the slowest thing this program does;
over a large archive or a network mount it is not worth waiting for merely to
look around. In shallow mode directory sizes read as unknown \(em a dash and an
empty bar, exactly as remote directories do \(em and sort last rather than
masquerading as small. The title bar reads \fBSHALLOW\fR so a tree of dashes is
never mistaken for a broken one.
.IP
Turning measuring back on asks first, since that is the walk you avoided. The
choice is remembered per directory in \fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR, so
somewhere you have decided not to measure opens that way next time. It can also
be set from the picker with \fBS\fR, without opening the directory at all.
.TP
.B Ctrl+r
Rescan the file tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl+p
Toggle the info panel sidebar.
.TP
.B gd
Open the picker: your saved directories and remote hosts in one list, with a
path field above it. \fBTab\fR switches between the two halves and the focused
one is outlined in cyan. In the list, \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR / \fBg\fR / \fBG\fR
navigate, \fBEnter\fR opens a directory or connects to a host depending on which
kind the cursor is on, \fBa\fR saves a destination, \fBe\fR edits one, \fBd\fR
forgets one, \fBp\fR / \fBu\fR pin and unpin, \fBm\fR reorders, \fBS\fR toggles
measuring and \fBEsc\fR closes.
.IP
\fB/\fR searches everything on screen. If the search narrows the list to a
single row, \fBEnter\fR opens it straight away; with more than one match
\fBEnter\fR keeps the narrowed list so you can pick from it.
.IP
The path field takes a local path or a remote
\fBsftp://[user@]host[:port][/path]\fR URL, so an address you have not saved
needs no separate step \(em type it and press \fBEnter\fR. A connection replaces
whatever the active panel was showing.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Release the mouse so the terminal's own text selection works; press again to
re-grab it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+t
Show or hide the transfer panel.
.TP
.B Tab
Rotates through every visible pane in turn \(em each browser panel from left to
right, then the transfer panel \(em and wraps back to the first. Only one pane
shows the bright focus border at a time. While the transfer
panel has focus, \fBj\fR / \fBK\fR move between transfers, \fBk\fR or
\fBDelete\fR cancels the selected one (after a confirmation), and \fBEsc\fR
returns to the tree. A double-click on a transfer also cancels it.
.TP
.B gx
Cancel all queued and in-flight transfers.
.SH REMOTE BROWSING
myd can browse and transfer files over SFTP. Connect from the \fBgd\fR picker
\(em pick a saved host, or type an \fBsftp://\fR URL into its path field \(em or
launch with a remote target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
myd sftp://prod
myd sftp://user@host:2222/var/log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authentication reuses your existing SSH setup: the ssh-agent, then keys under
\fB~/.ssh\fR (prompting for a passphrase if a key is encrypted), then a password
as a last resort. \fB~/.ssh/config\fR host aliases and \fB~/.ssh/known_hosts\fR
are honored. Credentials are never written to disk.
.PP
Copying (\fBc\fR) between a remote and a local panel runs as a background
transfer rather than blocking the interface. Up to sixteen transfers run at once
and further requests stack up. The transfer panel appears once you start a copy
(toggle it any time with \fBCtrl+t\fR) and shows each transfer's progress, rate,
and ETA. Large files download through many concurrent pipelined reads, and the
files and subdirectories within a directory copy transfer concurrently, so a
tree of small files is not paced by round-trip latency.
.PP
Creating directories (\fBN\fR), renaming (\fBR\fR), deleting (\fBD\fR) and
moving (\fBm\fR) are all routed through the backend the panel is browsing, so
they act on the remote host rather than the local machine.
.PP
Remote directory sizes cannot be measured cheaply: a \fBdu\fR-style walk over
SFTP costs one round trip per directory. Rather than report the directory
inode's own size as though it were a total, such directories show a dash
(\(em) with an empty size bar, and they sort as \fIunknown\fR \(em grouped
last \(em rather than as small. This holds whether or not the directory is
expanded: expanding reveals one level, not the whole subtree, so a running
total would grow with each expansion and never be the truth. The treemap
likewise gives unmeasured directories equal tiles instead of implying a size
comparison it cannot make.
.PP
The info panel shows only what the directory listing carries (size and
timestamps); owner, group and creation time are not part of it.
.SH SAVED HOSTS
Saved remote locations live in \fB~/.config/myd/hosts.toml\fR (honouring
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR) and are hand-editable. An entry records a label, host,
optional user, port and starting path, plus a use count and the time of the last
connection.
.PP
Hosts are listed after the directories in the \fBgd\fR picker, ordered strictly
by recency \(em most recently connected first \(em so the list changes visibly
as you work. The use count is displayed but does not affect the order.
.PP
.B No passwords are stored.
Authentication goes through the same ladder as always: ssh-agent, then
\fB~/.ssh\fR keys, then a prompt.
.PP
A malformed file is reported and ignored rather than overwritten, so a typo can
be corrected by hand instead of costing the whole list.
.SH MOUSE
The wheel scrolls the focused view; a left click focuses a panel and selects a
row or treemap tile; a right click selects and opens. Mouse capture takes over
the terminal's own click-drag selection, so \fBCtrl+N\fR releases and re-grabs
it. Most terminals also honour Shift+drag while captured.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Setting \fBMYD_TRACE=1\fR writes a log to \fB~/.cache/myd\-trace.log\fR (override
with \fBMYD_TRACE_FILE\fR). \fBMYD_LOG\fR takes a filter directive instead, e.g.
\fBMYD_LOG=myd::transfer=debug\fR for just the transfer engine;
\fBMYD_LOG_FORMAT=json\fR makes the output machine-readable.
.PP
The log records key and event-loop timings, connection setup, the SFTP read
limit the server negotiated, and one line per transferred file with the path
taken, chunk size, window depth and achieved rate. Per-chunk latencies are
accumulated into a histogram and summarised once per file, so tracing never
becomes the bottleneck it is measuring. It is off by default and costs nothing
when unset.
.SH TUNING TRANSFERS
Every performance-relevant setting is an environment variable, so a slow link
can be diagnosed without a rebuild.
.TP
.B MYD_SSH_WINDOW
SSH channel window in bytes (default 64 MiB). This is the hard ceiling on
in-flight data: throughput cannot exceed \fBwindow / rtt\fR however deep the
request pipeline is.
.TP
.B MYD_SSH_NODELAY
Disable Nagle's algorithm on the SSH socket (default true).
.TP
.B MYD_SFTP_WRITE_LIMIT
Outstanding bytes on the sequential write path (default 16 MiB).
.TP
.B MYD_SFTP_MAX_PENDING
SFTP requests in flight on one connection (default 1024).
.TP
.B MYD_CHUNK_SIZE
Bytes per chunk (default 256 KiB). On the parallel path one chunk is one wire
request, so a value above the server's negotiated read limit becomes several
serial requests rather than one larger one.
.TP
.B MYD_CHUNKS_IN_FLIGHT
Concurrent chunk reads within one large file (default 32).
.TP
.B MYD_MAX_PARALLEL
Concurrent transfers, and files per directory level (default 16).
.TP
.B MYD_GLOBAL_CONCURRENCY
Ceiling on concurrent operations during a recursive copy (default 192). Without
it the per-level windows multiply.
.SH SORT MODES
Press \fBs\fR to cycle through the available sort modes:
.TP
.B dirs-first
Directories are listed before files; alphabetical order within each group.
.TP
.B files-first
Files are listed before directories; alphabetical order within each group.
.TP
.B largest
Items sorted by descending size. Directories are measured recursively.
.TP
.B smallest
Items sorted by ascending size. Directories are measured recursively.
.TP
.B newest
Items sorted by modification time, most recent first.
.TP
.B oldest
Items sorted by modification time, least recent first.
.TP
.B recently-accessed
Items sorted by access time, most recent first.
.PP
The time-based sorts use the timestamps from the directory listing, so they work
identically on local and remote (SFTP) trees with no extra latency.
.SH SYMLINKS
Symbolic links are shown with a distinct link icon, a cyan italic name, and a
trailing \fB@\fR (\fB@/\fR when the link points at a directory), so they remain
distinguishable on terminals without color.
.PP
A symlink to a directory can be entered and expanded exactly like a real
directory, on both local and remote (SFTP) trees. Sizes and timestamps shown for
a link are those of its target. A broken link keeps the values reported by the
directory listing.
.SH SIZE BARS
Each entry in the tree displays a proportional size bar indicating the item's
share of its parent directory's total content. The bar is color-coded:
.TP
.B Green
The item occupies less than 50% of its parent's total size.
.TP
.B Amber
The item occupies 50\-80% of its parent's total size.
.TP
.B Red
The item occupies more than 80% of its parent's total size.
.PP
Toggle the size bars on and off with \fBb\fR.
.SH TREEMAP VIEW
Press \fBv\fR to switch between the file tree and a squarified treemap that
visualizes disk usage as proportional rectangular tiles: larger files and
directories occupy more area. Move between tiles with \fBh\fR, \fBj\fR,
\fBk\fR, and \fBl\fR; press \fBl\fR or \fBEnter\fR to descend into a directory.
.PP
Each tile is filled with a color for its content category: code, docs, images,
video, audio, archives, data, binaries, or other. A file's category comes from
its extension; a directory takes the category holding most of its bytes. A
legend in the status bar names the categories currently on screen. When a tile
is too narrow to show its full name, the selected item's name is shown in the
status bar.
.SH INFO PANEL
The right-hand sidebar (hidden by default; toggle with \fBCtrl+p\fR)
displays detailed metadata for the currently selected item:
.TP
.B Name
The file or directory name.
.TP
.B Type
Whether the item is a file or directory.
.TP
.B Size
Human-readable file size (e.g., "245 MB").
.TP
.B Contents
For directories: the count of files and subdirectories.
.TP
.B Permissions
Standard Unix permission string (e.g., \fBdrwxr-xr-x\fR).
.TP
.B Owner / Group
The owning user and group.
.TP
.B Timestamps
Creation, modification, and last access times.
.TP
.B Path
The absolute path to the item.
.PP
Hide or show the panel with \fBCtrl+p\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B myd
Start with the directory picker.
.TP
.B myd ~/Documents
Start browsing at \fB~/Documents\fR.
.TP
.B myd /var/log
Start browsing at \fB/var/log\fR.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.TP
.B Rust
1.75 or later (stable).
.TP
.B Terminal
A terminal with true color (256-color or 24-bit) support.
.SH INSTALLATION
.PP
.RS
.nf
cargo install \-\-path .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or run directly without installation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cargo run
cargo run \-\- ~/Documents
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vi (1),
.BR tree (1),
.BR lf (1)
